  "teaser": "From Birmingham to Canterbury, British soul didn't copy America — it absorbed it, melted it down, and found something entirely its own.",
  "description": "There's a story about British soul that rarely gets told straight. Not imitation — absorption. A group from Birmingham in 1978 who sounded like they'd grown up on Gamble and Huff. A London trio who actually broke through in America without a gimmick. A voice from Canterbury who sang as if the microphone wasn't on, recording neo-soul before anyone had named it.\n\nLew Kirton is in here too — British soul with Barbadian roots, which makes it personal for me. Same part of the world my mum came from.\n\nThe show ends in 2024, with a voice that knows exactly where it came from. The circle closes. It always does.\n\n— Simone",
  "quote": "Omar, from Canterbury, released There's Nothing Like This in 1990, and it sounded like it had been recorded in a bedroom in America in 1972.",
  "home_quote": {
    "text": "Neo-soul before anyone had named it. *Omar.*",
    "context": "On Omar, There's Nothing Like This"
  },
